From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: athexweb3 <athexweb3@nirapod.org>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 13:04:25 +0600
Subject: [PATCH] armv8a: add the SIMD&FP registers

On armv8a, xreg_size is U128 but there are no xregisters, so a u128
value can't live in a register. The 32 SIMD&FP registers v0-v31 now
form the xreg class, modeled at their full 128 bits.

They're allocatable and part of the calling convention, as in AAPCS64:
arguments go in v0-v7, results in v0-v3, and v8-v15 are callee-saved.
AAPCS64 only requires the low 64 bits of v8-v15 to be preserved, but
they're saved and restored as whole Q registers, so each register has
one size. MOV (vector) and LDR and STR of a Q register are added, and
u128 moves, loads and stores are lowered to them. The intrinsics are
named V followed by the assembly mnemonic (#VMOV, #VLDR, #VSTR). All
three are data-independent-time instructions (DDI 0487 M.d, C5.2.4).

A Q load or store takes an immediate offset that is a multiple of 16
from 0 to 65520 or lies in [-256, 255], or an unshifted register. The
to-save check already requires Q save slots to be 16-byte aligned, so
the save-slot offset test stays conservative for them, and an
X17-relative offset that can't be encoded is rejected at assembly
generation.

armv8a_lloads gets a branch that is never taken: restoring SP from a
128-bit variable. The generic lloads specification covers any rspi
whose type passes lip_check_ws, which now includes U128, so the
definition needs that case, but the only caller passes the stack
pointer.

On the C side, a u128 argument is a short vector type such as
uint8x16_t, not __int128, which AAPCS64 passes in a pair of X
registers.

Since the previous commit, system calls aren't assumed to preserve the
vector registers. So an export that issues #randombytes now saves and
restores q8-q15 in full, which costs 16 instructions and 128 bytes of
frame, and a u128 can't stay in a register across the call. There are
no D, S, H or B views of the registers, and no #protect for u128.
#[callee_saved = n] can be rejected when X and Q registers are mixed,
a problem the hint already has on main. A reg u256 is rejected before
allocation by a new generic check, whose error says that the vector
bank has no register of that type. Without it, the u256 would be given
a Q register and fail later with an internal error.

The 32 new registers shift the identifiers of the variables created
after them, so register allocation can break ties differently on every
architecture. The resulting allocations are equivalent.

Co-authored-by: orvenweb3 <orvenweb3@gmail.com>
